## Find the pattern in the lengths removed
Each step removes the middle third of every remaining segment. The number of segments doubles and each is one third as long.
\text{Step 2} &= 2 \times \frac{1}{9}
&= \frac{2}{9}
## Step 3: 4 segments, each of length 1/27
\text{Step 3} &= 4 \times \frac{1}{27}
&= \frac{4}{27}
## Step 4: 8 segments, each of length 1/81
\text{Step 4} &= 8 \times \frac{1}{81}
&= \frac{8}{81}
## Step 5: 16 segments, each of length 1/243
\text{Step 5} &= 16 \times \frac{1}{243}
&= \frac{16}{243}
ANSWER: \text{Step 3: } \tfrac{4}{27},\ \text{Step 4: } \tfrac{8}{81},\ \text{Step 5: } \tfrac{16}{243}

## Spot the geometric series
The lengths removed are $\frac13, \frac29, \frac4{27}, \dots$, a geometric series.
a &= \frac{1}{3}
r &= \frac{2/9}{1/3} = \frac{2}{3}
## Substitute into the sum formula
S_n &= \frac{a(1-r^n)}{1-r}
S_n &= \frac{\frac{1}{3}\left(1-\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^n\right)}{1-\frac{2}{3}}
S_n &= \frac{\frac{1}{3}\left(1-\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^n\right)}{\frac{1}{3}}
S_n &= 1-\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^n
ANSWER: S_n = 1-\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)^n

## Use the sum to infinity
Since $|r| = \frac23 < 1$, the sum to infinity exists.
S_\infty &= \frac{a}{1-r}
&= \frac{\frac{1}{3}}{1-\frac{2}{3}}
&= \frac{\frac{1}{3}}{\frac{1}{3}}
&= 1
ANSWER: 1

## Find A from step 1
Step 1 removes the open interval $\left(\frac13, \frac23\right)$, so the second segment starts at $\frac23$.
A &= \frac{2}{3}
## Find B, C and D from step 2
Step 2 removes $\left(\frac19, \frac29\right)$ and $\left(\frac79, \frac89\right)$.
B &= \frac{2}{9}
C &= \frac{7}{9}
D &= \frac{8}{9}
## Find E from step 3
The segment $\left[\frac{6}{27}, \frac{9}{27}\right]$ has length $\frac{3}{27}$. Step 3 removes its middle third $\left(\frac{7}{27}, \frac{8}{27}\right)$, leaving $\left[\frac{6}{27}, \frac{7}{27}\right]$ on the left.
E &= \frac{6}{27}+\frac{1}{27}
&= \frac{7}{27}
## Find F from step 3
The segment $\left[\frac{24}{27}, 1\right]$ has its middle third $\left(\frac{25}{27}, \frac{26}{27}\right)$ removed, leaving $\left[\frac{24}{27}, \frac{25}{27}\right]$ on the left.
F &= \frac{24}{27}+\frac{1}{27}
&= \frac{25}{27}
ANSWER: A=\tfrac23,\ B=\tfrac29,\ C=\tfrac79,\ D=\tfrac89,\ E=\tfrac7{27},\ F=\tfrac{25}{27}

## Add the fractions over a common denominator
\frac{1}{3}-\frac{1}{9}+\frac{1}{27}-\frac{1}{81} &= \frac{27}{81}-\frac{9}{81}+\frac{3}{81}-\frac{1}{81}
&= \frac{20}{81}
## Show that this point is never removed
After step 3 the segment $\left[\frac{18}{81}, \frac{21}{81}\right]$ remains. Step 4 removes its middle third $\left(\frac{19}{81}, \frac{20}{81}\right)$, leaving $\left[\frac{20}{81}, \frac{21}{81}\right]$.
So $\frac{20}{81}$ is an end-point of a segment. End-points are never removed, so it is in the Cantor Set.
ANSWER: \tfrac{20}{81}\text{ is an end-point of a segment, so it is never removed}

## Identify the geometric series
a &= \frac{1}{3}
r &= \frac{-1/9}{1/3} = -\frac{1}{3}
$|r| = \frac13 < 1$, so the sum to infinity exists.
## Substitute into the sum to infinity
S_\infty &= \frac{a}{1-r}
&= \frac{\frac{1}{3}}{1-\left(-\frac{1}{3}\right)}
&= \frac{\frac{1}{3}}{1+\frac{1}{3}}
&= \frac{\frac{1}{3}}{\frac{4}{3}}
&= \frac{1}{4}
ANSWER: \frac{1}{4}

## Set r(t) = 26250 and isolate the cosine
22500\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{26}t\right)+37500 &= 26250
22500\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{26}t\right) &= -11250
\cos\left(\frac{\pi}{26}t\right) &= -\frac{1}{2}
## Find the angles in the first 52 weeks
For $0 \le t \le 52$, the angle $\frac{\pi}{26}t$ runs from $0$ to $2\pi$. Cosine is $-\frac12$ in the second and third quadrants.
\frac{\pi}{26}t &= \frac{2\pi}{3} \text{ or } \frac{4\pi}{3}
## Solve for the first value of t
\frac{\pi}{26}t &= \frac{2\pi}{3}
t &= \frac{2\pi}{3}\times\frac{26}{\pi}
t &= \frac{52}{3}
## Solve for the second value of t
\frac{\pi}{26}t &= \frac{4\pi}{3}
t &= \frac{4\pi}{3}\times\frac{26}{\pi}
t &= \frac{104}{3}
ANSWER: t = \frac{52}{3} \text{ and } t = \frac{104}{3}

## Put P = 12 and make y the subject
2x + 2y + \pi x &= 12
2y &= 12 - 2x - \pi x
y &= \frac{12 - 2x - \pi x}{2}
y &= \frac{12 - (2+\pi)x}{2}
## Find the range of x
The height $y$ cannot be negative, and the radius $x$ cannot be negative.
12 - (2+\pi)x &\ge 0
(2+\pi)x &\le 12
x &\le \frac{12}{2+\pi}
So $0 \le x \le \frac{12}{2+\pi}$.
ANSWER: y = \frac{12-(2+\pi)x}{2}

## Complete the table: x = 0
y &= \frac{12-(2+\pi)(0)}{2}
&= \frac{12}{2}
&= 6
## Complete the table: x = 12/(2+π)
y &= \frac{12-(2+\pi)\cdot\frac{12}{2+\pi}}{2}
&= \frac{12-12}{2}
&= 0
## Plot the two points and join them
The table gives $(0, 6)$ and $\left(\frac{12}{2+\pi}, 0\right) \approx (2.33, 0)$. The function is linear, so join them with a straight line, drawn only for $0 \le x \le \frac{12}{2+\pi}$.
ANSWER: (0, 6) \text{ and } \left(\frac{12}{2+\pi}, 0\right)

## Add the areas of the rectangle and semicircle
The rectangle is $2x$ wide and $y$ high. The semicircle has radius $x$.
a &= 2xy + \frac{\pi x^2}{2}
## Substitute y and simplify
a &= 2x\cdot\frac{12-(2+\pi)x}{2} + \frac{\pi x^2}{2}
&= x\left(12-(2+\pi)x\right) + \frac{\pi x^2}{2}
&= 12x - 2x^2 - \pi x^2 + \frac{\pi x^2}{2}
&= \frac{24x - 4x^2 - 2\pi x^2}{2} + \frac{\pi x^2}{2}
&= \frac{24x - 4x^2 - 2\pi x^2 + \pi x^2}{2}
&= \frac{24x - (\pi+4)x^2}{2}
ANSWER: a(x) = \frac{24x - (\pi+4)x^2}{2}

## Set a'(x) = 0 to find x
a'(x) &= 0
12 - (\pi+4)x &= 0
(\pi+4)x &= 12
x &= \frac{12}{\pi+4}
This is a maximum because $a''(x) = -(\pi+4) < 0$.
## Substitute x into the formula for y
y &= \frac{12-(2+\pi)\left(\frac{12}{\pi+4}\right)}{2}
&= \frac{\frac{12(\pi+4)}{\pi+4}-\frac{12(2+\pi)}{\pi+4}}{2}
&= \frac{12(\pi+4)-12(2+\pi)}{2(\pi+4)}
&= \frac{12\pi+48-24-12\pi}{2(\pi+4)}
&= \frac{24}{2(\pi+4)}
&= \frac{12}{\pi+4}
## Compare x and y
Both $x$ and $y$ equal $\frac{12}{\pi+4} \approx 1.68$, so $y = x$: the area is greatest when the height of the rectangle equals the radius of the semicircle.
ANSWER: y = x

## Boy first, then girl
There are 20 students: 12 boys and 8 girls. After one is chosen, 19 remain.
P(\text{boy, girl}) &= \frac{12}{20}\times\frac{8}{19}
&= \frac{96}{380}
## Girl first, then boy
P(\text{girl, boy}) &= \frac{8}{20}\times\frac{12}{19}
&= \frac{96}{380}
## Add the two cases
A boy and a girl in any order is either boy then girl, or girl then boy.
P &= \frac{96}{380} + \frac{96}{380}
&= \frac{192}{380}
&= \frac{48}{95}
ANSWER: \frac{48}{95}

## Write each probability
Students are not replaced, so the total drops by 1 each time. The boys left also drop with each boy chosen.
P(\text{1st boy}) &= \frac{12}{20}
P(\text{2nd boy}) &= \frac{11}{19}
P(\text{3rd boy}) &= \frac{10}{18}
P(\text{4th girl}) &= \frac{8}{17}
## Multiply the four probabilities
P &= \frac{12}{20}\times\frac{11}{19}\times\frac{10}{18}\times\frac{8}{17}
&= \frac{12\times 11\times 10\times 8}{20\times 19\times 18\times 17}
&= \frac{10560}{116280}
## Simplify by dividing top and bottom by 120
&= \frac{88}{969}
ANSWER: \frac{88}{969}

## Find P(F) and use independence
P(F) &= \frac{1}{4} + \frac{1}{5}
&= \frac{9}{20}
Independent events satisfy $P(F \cap S) = P(F) \times P(S)$.
\frac{1}{5} &= \frac{9}{20} \times P(S)
## Solve for P(S)
P(S) &= \frac{1}{5} \times \frac{20}{9}
&= \frac{4}{9}
## Find x
$P(S)$ is the overlap plus the part of $S$ outside $F$.
x + \frac{1}{5} &= \frac{4}{9}
x &= \frac{4}{9} - \frac{1}{5}
&= \frac{20}{45} - \frac{9}{45}
&= \frac{11}{45}
## Find y
All four regions of the Venn diagram add to 1.
\frac{1}{4} + \frac{1}{5} + \frac{11}{45} + y &= 1
y &= 1 - \frac{1}{4} - \frac{1}{5} - \frac{11}{45}
&= \frac{180}{180} - \frac{45}{180} - \frac{36}{180} - \frac{44}{180}
&= \frac{55}{180}
&= \frac{11}{36}
ANSWER: x = \frac{11}{45}, \quad y = \frac{11}{36}

## Choose the ratio in triangle ADO
$|OD|$ is adjacent to $\angle DOA$ and $|OA|$ is the hypotenuse, so use cosine.
\cos(\angle DOA) &= \frac{|OD|}{|OA|}
&= \frac{60}{90}
&= \frac{2}{3}
## Take the inverse cosine (calculator in radians)
|\angle DOA| &= \cos^{-1}\left(\frac{2}{3}\right)
&= 0.8411
&\approx 0.84
ANSWER: 0.84 \text{ radians}

## Find the volume of sand left
98% has flowed out, so 2% is left, all in the cone.
V_{\text{left}} &= 0.02 \times 23.73
&= 0.4746
## Use similar triangles to write r in terms of h
The sand surface cone has the same shape as the full cone (radius $1.25$, height $1.5$).
\frac{r}{h} &= \frac{1.25}{1.5}
&= \frac{5}{6}
r &= \frac{5h}{6}
## Write the cone volume in terms of h
V &= \frac{1}{3}\pi r^2 h
&= \frac{1}{3}\pi\left(\frac{5h}{6}\right)^2 h
&= \frac{25\pi h^3}{108}
## Set the volume equal to 0.4746 and solve for h
\frac{25\pi h^3}{108} &= 0.4746
h^3 &= \frac{0.4746 \times 108}{25\pi}
&= 0.65262
h &= \sqrt[3]{0.65262}
&= 0.8674
&\approx 0.87
ANSWER: h = 0.87 \text{ cm}
